body.translated_text:
Baudh1.5.8.23-1/ And now they cite (the following):
Baudh1.5.8.23-2ab/ A <s>brāhmaṇa</s> is pure when the water reaches the heart; a <s>kṣatriya</s> when it reaches the throat;
Baudh1.5.8.23-2cd/ a <s>vaiśya</s> is purified by water that has been tasted; and a woman or <s>śūdra</s> by merely touching (the water) — thus.

Baudh2.1.1.6-1/ And they also cite:
Baudh2.1.1.6-2/ Having killed a <s>Brāhmaṇa</s> unintentionally, one becomes guilty according to <s>dharma</s> (law);
the sages declare an expiation for one who acted without intention;
but for one who kills intentionally, no expiation is found.

Baudh2.1.1.7/ If one injures another, one should perform <s>kṛcchra</s> (severe penance); for striking down, <s>atikṛcchra</s> (extreme penance);
for causing bloodshed, <s>kṛcchra</s> and <s>cāndrāyaṇa</s> (lunar penance);
therefore one should neither injure nor cause bloodshed.

Baudh2.3.5.7-1/ Now they also cite the following:
Baudh2.3.5.7-2ab/ Having drawn out three <s>piṇḍas</s> (offering balls), one may do so in emergencies but not always;
Baudh2.3.5.7-2cd/ when the waters are obstructed, one should use clay balls from a well and three pots of water. Thus it is said.

Baudh2.4.7.3-1/ And moreover they cite:
Baudh2.4.7.3-2ab/ Bathing by immersion in water is prescribed for all <s>varṇas</s> (social classes);
Baudh2.4.7.3-2cd/ but sprinkling accompanied by mantras is especially distinguished for the twice-born — thus it is stated.

Baudh2.5.8.3-1/ Then he approaches the waters:
Baudh2.5.8.3-2ab/ "I take refuge in <s>Varuṇa</s> of the golden horns; grant me a <s>tīrtha</s> (sacred ford), I beseech you;
Baudh2.5.8.3-2cd/ whatever I have eaten from the unworthy, and gifts received from sinful persons —"

Baudh2.7.12.11: Having performed <s>ācamana</s> (sipping) again, he lets the water flow over [his] right big toe [reciting]:
The <s>Puruṣa</s> (Person) is of the measure of a thumb, and resides in the thumb;
the lord of the entire universe, the master, the all-consuming one, is pleased.

Baudh2.10.17.7ab: This is the eternal greatness of a <s>brāhmaṇa</s> — it is neither increased nor diminished by action;
Baudh2.10.17.7cd: the self alone knows the path to it; having known it, one is not tainted by evil action. (cf. Baudh2.6.11.30)
Baudh2.10.17.8: [It is called] 'eternal' because it leads to non-return [to rebirth].

Baudh4.1.3: One should perform <s>prāṇāyāmas</s> (breath-restraints) according to the procedure prescribed in the scriptures,
for whatever sin is committed by the genitals, or what is done by the feet,
by the arms, by the mind, by speech, by the ears, the skin, the nose, or the eyes.

Baudh4.1.14: For three years after reaching puberty, a maiden should await her father's command;
then in the fourth year she should find a husband of equal [standing] for herself.
If one of equal standing is not available, she may resort even to one of inferior qualities.

Baudh4.2.15: Or, fasting for three nights, entering water three times a day [K: one should enter the water three times a day],
restraining the vital breaths within oneself, one should recite the <s>Aghamarṣaṇa</s> [hymn] three times;
this is like the concluding bath of the <s>Aśvamedha</s> (horse sacrifice) — so <s>Manu</s> declared.

Baudh4.2.16-1: And it is known [from revelation]:
Baudh4.2.16-2: The observance [of dharma] is a purifier, widespread, ancient — by which, once purified, one crosses over evil deeds;
by that purifier, cleansed and purified, may we pass beyond sin and enmity — thus [it is declared].

Baudh4.5.13: The portion of cow's urine is [one measure]; half of that is cow dung; milk is three [measures] [K: of milk, three];
curds is two; ghee is one; and <s>kuśa</s>-grass water is one.
Thus the <s>sāṃtapana kṛcchra</s> (scorching penance) would purify even a <s>śvapāka</s> (outcaste).

Baudh4.8.14: This sacred [teaching] of <s>Prajāpati</s> [K: <s>Prajāpati</s>], proclaimed by the sage [K: of the sages], the destroyer of sin —
one should teach this always, retain it, or even [merely] hear it;
one is freed from all sins and is glorified in the world of <s>Brahman</s>.
